{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The claimed reduction does not obviously follow, and the refutation conditions reveal the failure: SRC_001's registry-size account would be refuted exactly when guarded modules had many mutations AND the asymmetry persisted—but SRC_004's order-effect account would still explain that persistence as a sequence artifact (wrong installation order preventing manifestation of the selective effect despite high mutation density). Thus the size account and the order account are orthogonal rather than reductive: size determines whether a selective effect is measurable, order determines whether the mutation stream allows it to manifest. The target presupposes that size acts as the determinant of order's efficacy, but a high-mutation module can still exhibit order-dependent outcomes, proving the order effect operates independently of size and is not merely a special case of the size constraint.","counterexample":["Guarded module G has 150 mutations (high size) but they are all proposed before protective guards are installed; per SRC_004's order effect, no selective pressure manifests, so G still shows 4/4 asymmetry—demonstrating the order effect operates independently of size and is not merely a special case of the size constraint."],"premise":"If guarded modules had dozens of mutations and still lost 4/4 to 6/7, the order-effect explanation (SRC_004) would still account for the asymmetry as a sequence artifact (guards installed too late or in wrong order), whereas this same evidence refutes the registry-size account (SRC_001) according to the target's own stated conditions.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"a1b2c3d4e5f6","quote":"Refuted if ordinarily-guarded modules each had dozens of mutations and still lost 4/4 to 6/7, or if mutation proposal and guard installation order were randomized across the project and the asymmetry persisted."}],"target":"37c89cb1f7914bd68bf0f76ecfee95db2bf50adf82499138befcb87d74d68e33"}]}